Data Cleaning & Preprocessing Summary

2.1 Data Cleaning and Preparation
To ensure that the analysis accurately reflects retail investor attention and discussion patterns on WallStreetBets during the 2020–2021 period, we implemented a structured data cleaning and preprocessing pipeline focused on improving data consistency, removing redundancy, and preparing features relevant to engagement analysis.

First, the time-related variables were standardized by converting the timestamps into datetime format and comparing it with the original unix time field. Redundant time columns were removed, and additional temporal features such as posting hour, month, and day of week were extracted to support analysis of when investor discussions are most active.
Second, we conducted a comprehensive duplicate and near-duplicate assessment. This included checking for repeated rows, duplicate post IDs, and repeated titles. In addition, semantic similarity checks based on TF-IDF and cosine similarity were used to detect highly similar posts that could artificially inflate engagement patterns. These steps help ensure that observed discussion trends are not driven by duplicated or redundant content.
For textual data, the title and post body were cleaned to improve consistency and reduce noise. The cleaning process included converting text to lowercase, removing URLs and markdown artifacts, decoding HTML entities, and normalizing whitespace. These steps allow more reliable extraction of textual features and prevent formatting issues from affecting the analysis.
Since many Reddit posts do not contain body text,the missing body values were treated as structured rather than random. Instead of imputing values, we created a binary indicator variable (has_body) to capture whether a post contains additional text content. Missing bodies were replaced with empty strings to maintain compatibility with text processing while preserving the informational value of the presence og the body.
Finally, engagement variables such as post score and number of comments exhibit highly skewed, heavy-tailed distributions due to the presence of viral posts. Instead of removing these influential observations, we applied log transformations to stabilize the distributions and enable more interpretable comparisons across posts. Additional preparation steps included grouping rare post types into a common “Other” category and creating NLP-ready text fields for potential downstream analysis.

Overall, these preprocessing steps improve data quality while preserving meaningful signals about how retail investors discuss and respond to investment ideas on WallStreetBets.
